    <region left="18" top="4127" width="760" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Plots</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4161" width="760" height="27" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Charts generated in MS Excel (Lab_09_-_Airfoil_Lift_Plots.xlsx) and pasted here. Error bars show ± uncertainty.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4196" width="760" height="27" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Part A: measured wind tunnel velocity vs. β</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4231" width="720" height="449" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4696" width="760" height="27" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Part B: measured and NASA reference lift coefficient vs. angle of attack</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="4731" width="720" height="449" color="#000000">
      <picture>
        <raw format="png" encoding="base64"></raw>
      </picture>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="5206" width="760" height="26" color="#000000" fontSize="12">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p style="font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold;">Q/A</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="5240" width="760" height="163" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Advantages and disadvantages of each technique: The hot wire anemometer is small, responds quickly, and reads velocity directly with fine resolution, but the wire is fragile, it must be calibrated, and it is sensitive to air temperature and to probe orientation; in our data it read higher than the other two instruments and even gave a higher reading at β = 0.9 than at β = 1.0. The turbine (vane) anemometer is inexpensive, rugged, and easy to read, but its relatively large vane averages the flow over its area, disturbs the flow, and has friction that makes it under-read at low speeds. The Pitot-static probe has no moving parts, needs no calibration, and is the standard reference for airspeed (it is used on aircraft), but it must be aligned with the flow, it requires a manometer and a density calculation, and the small manometer heights at low speeds are hard to read precisely, which gives it the largest uncertainty of the three.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="5411" width="760" height="146" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Comparison with NASA: our measured lift coefficients were 9% to 24% lower than the NASA reference values at the same angles of attack. The general trend matched (the lift coefficient rose roughly linearly with angle of attack), but our values were lower and the airfoil stalled earlier, near 10°, instead of about 14° to 16°. The main reasons are: (1) Reynolds number - our test ran at about Re = 98,875, while the NASA data were taken at 3 to 9 million, and lift falls and stall occurs earlier at low Reynolds numbers; (2) the NASA data are for a two-dimensional section (infinite span), while our airfoil has a finite span with tip vortices and wall gaps that reduce lift; (3) the rough surface and layer lines of the 3D-printed airfoil; (4) blockage and non-uniform flow in the small wind tunnel; and (5) friction or contact on the scale and rods, plus angle and manometer reading errors.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="5565" width="760" height="44" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Reynolds number at the trailing edge: Re_L ≈ 98,875. This is below the flat-plate transition Reynolds number of about 5 × 10^5, so the boundary layer at the trailing edge is laminar.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
    <region left="18" top="5617" width="760" height="95" color="#000000" fontSize="10">
      <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
        <content>
          <p>Lift on a full-scale wing: using our measured lift coefficient at 6° (C_L ≈ 0.786), a Cessna 172-sized wing (4.83 ft chord × 36 ft span = 173.9 ft²) at a takeoff speed of 55 knots (28.3 m/s) and the lab air density would produce a lift force of about 6,024 N, or about 1,354 lbf. This is less than a Cessna 172's maximum takeoff weight of about 2,450 lbf, because our measured lift coefficient is lower than the NASA value (which would give about 1,758 lbf); a real Cessna also uses flaps and a higher angle of attack at rotation.</p>
        </content>
      </text>
    </region>
  </regions>
</worksheet>
